    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Hard freezes are uncommon in Corpus Christi, but February 2021 brought daily record lows of 25°F, 17°F, and 19°F on February 14, 15, and 16.</li>
<li>Many homeowners and commercial policies cover sudden water damage from a burst pipe, subject to conditions such as maintaining heat or draining the system.</li>
<li>Burst pipe water is usually a water damage claim, not a flood claim. The source of the water matters.</li>
<li>Stop the water, photograph everything, and start drying quickly. Keep the failed pipe section if you can.</li>
<li>NOAA records can help tie the pipe failure to a specific freeze.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Most years, freezing weather in Corpus Christi is brief and mild. That is exactly why <strong>freeze damage and burst pipe claims in Corpus Christi</strong> catch so many owners off guard. Homes and businesses on the Coastal Bend are built for heat, humidity, and wind, and plumbing is not always protected the way it would be farther north. When a hard freeze does arrive, the damage often shows up after the thaw, when water starts running from a split pipe in a wall, attic, or slab.</p>

<p>This guide looks at what the February 2021 winter storm showed, how burst pipe claims are usually handled, and how to document a freeze loss. It is general information. Coverage depends on your policy and the facts of your loss.</p>

<h2>What February 2021 looked like in Corpus Christi</h2>

<p>The National Weather Service office in Corpus Christi refers to the event as the "South Texas Arctic Outbreak - February 2021." Daily temperatures from NOAA's threaded climate record for the Corpus Christi area, based at Corpus Christi International Airport, tell the story:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Low temperature</th><th>Previous daily record low</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>February 14, 2021</td><td>25°F</td><td>26°F (1895)</td><td>New daily record low</td></tr>
<tr><td>February 15, 2021</td><td>17°F</td><td>25°F (1895)</td><td>New daily record low; high of 31°F, below freezing all day</td></tr>
<tr><td>February 16, 2021</td><td>19°F</td><td>20°F (1895)</td><td>New daily record low</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>These are daily records for each date, not the all-time record low for Corpus Christi. Even so, three straight days of record cold, with one full day below freezing, is the kind of stretch that can freeze water lines in attics, exterior walls, and crawl spaces that rarely see that kind of cold.</p>

<p>The water system was affected too. The Texas Tribune reported that a city-wide boil-water notice was issued in Corpus Christi during the storm. Local news reporting by KRIS 6 said the state environmental agency (TCEQ) required the notice on February 16 and that it was lifted early Saturday, February 20, 2021, after nearly five days. We cite those details as news reports.</p>

<h3>Freezes still happen</h3>
<p>February 2021 was exceptional, but it was not the last freeze. NOAA's Storm Events Database lists Frost/Freeze entries for Nueces County in January 2026, with the Corpus Christi airport (KCRP) and Robstown (KRBO) reporting temperatures of 32°F or less for at least two hours on January 25 and 26, 2026 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1314273" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event 1314273</a>), and 0.03 inches of freezing rain measured at KCRP on January 25 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1314304" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event 1314304</a>). The database lists 12 Frost/Freeze reports for Nueces County in total. Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page shows more of these records.</p>

<h2>Why pipes burst in Coastal Bend buildings</h2>

<p>Water expands as it freezes. In a closed pipe, that expansion builds pressure, and the pipe often splits somewhere between the ice blockage and a closed faucet. The leak may not appear until the ice melts and water pressure returns. Common trouble spots in Corpus Christi homes and businesses include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Attic plumbing.</strong> In some slab-on-grade homes, water lines run through the attic, where they can freeze when the attic gets cold.</li>
<li><strong>Exterior walls.</strong> Lines in uninsulated or poorly insulated exterior walls, especially on the north side of a building.</li>
<li><strong>Hose bibs and exposed piping.</strong> Outdoor faucets, irrigation backflow devices, pool equipment, and water heater lines in garages or closets on exterior walls.</li>
<li><strong>Elevated homes.</strong> On Padre Island, Mustang Island, and other areas with raised homes, piping under the living floor can be exposed to wind and cold.</li>
<li><strong>Vacant or seasonal properties.</strong> Rentals and second homes where the heat was off or set low.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How insurance policies usually handle burst pipes</h2>

<p>Every policy is different, but most homeowners and commercial policies follow a similar pattern. The table below summarizes common treatment. Your own policy language controls.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of damage</th><th>How it is often treated</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sudden water damage from a burst pipe (drywall, flooring, cabinets, contents)</td><td>Often covered as a sudden and accidental discharge, subject to policy conditions</td></tr>
<tr><td>The cost to repair the broken pipe itself</td><td>May be treated differently from the resulting water damage; some policies pay for access (tear-out) but not the pipe</td></tr>
<tr><td>Freeze damage where heat was not maintained or the system was not drained</td><td>Some policies limit or exclude freeze losses unless reasonable care was used, especially in vacant or unoccupied buildings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Long-term leaks or seepage</td><td>Often excluded if the leak continued over an extended period</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mold</td><td>Frequently limited or excluded; some policies give limited coverage when mold follows a covered water loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional living expenses or business income</td><td>May be covered if the property cannot be used while repairs are made, depending on the policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two things come up often on freeze claims. First, the insurer may ask what steps were taken to keep the building heated or the water shut off. Be honest and specific: if the power was out, say so and document it. Second, the insurer may look at how long the water was leaking. Fast reporting and clear timelines help.</p>

<h3>Burst pipes are not flood</h3>
<p>Water from inside the plumbing system is generally a water damage claim. Flood generally means surface water from outside the building, and flood coverage comes from NFIP or private flood policies, not standard homeowners policies. Our article on <a href="/water-damage-vs-flood-damage-corpus-christi/">water damage vs. flood damage in Corpus Christi</a> explains the difference in more detail.</p>

