<h2>What to do right after a freeze-related leak</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Shut off the water.</strong> Know where your main shutoff is before the next freeze. If the water heater or a branch line is involved, isolate it if you can.</li>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Turn off electricity to wet areas if water is near outlets or panels, and stay out of rooms with sagging ceilings.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything.</strong> Capture the water, the source, the affected rooms, and the contents before cleanup.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the failed pipe.</strong> Ask the plumber to save the broken section and write down where it was and what caused the failure.</li>
<li><strong>Start drying.</strong> Remove standing water and begin drying promptly. Wet drywall, insulation, and flooring can lead to mold within days. Keep receipts for fans, dehumidifiers, and mitigation services.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly.</strong> Give the date you discovered the leak and the date of the freeze, and keep a written record of who you spoke to.</li>
<li><strong>Do not throw out damaged items</strong> until they are documented, and ask the adjuster before disposing of large items.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Documenting a freeze damage claim</h2>

<p>Freeze claims are easier to resolve when the record clearly links the damage to the cold weather and shows the extent of the water damage. Useful documentation includes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Weather records</strong> for the date, such as NOAA daily temperatures and Storm Events entries. Our <a href="/weather-events/">weather events</a> page collects recent National Weather Service reports for the area.</li>
<li><strong>Plumber invoices</strong> that describe the failure, the location, and the cause.</li>
<li><strong>Moisture readings and drying logs</strong> from a mitigation company, showing which areas were wet and how drying progressed.</li>
<li><strong>Power outage records</strong> if the heat failed because the electricity was out.</li>
<li><strong>A contents list</strong> of damaged furniture, electronics, and personal property, with ages and costs where possible.</li>
<li><strong>A repair estimate</strong> that covers tear-out, drying, replacement of drywall, insulation, flooring, cabinets, and paint, plus any matching issues.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through the full list.</p>

<h2>Common disputes on burst pipe claims</h2>

<p>When freeze claims run into trouble, it is usually over one of these questions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Was the damage sudden or long-term?</strong> Evidence tying the failure to a specific freeze helps.</li>
<li><strong>Was reasonable care taken?</strong> Records of thermostat settings, occupancy, and power outages matter, especially for rentals and second homes.</li>
<li><strong>How much needs to be replaced?</strong> Disagreements about flooring that runs continuously through several rooms, cabinet replacement, or drying scope are common.</li>
<li><strong>Is mold covered?</strong> Mold limits and exclusions vary. Our <a href="/services/mold-damage-claims/">mold damage claims</a> page explains how mold usually fits into a water claim.</li>
</ul>

<p>If a claim is denied or underpaid, ask for the reason in writing and the specific policy language relied on. If the dispute is over the amount of loss, appraisal may be available. Our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied claim help</a> page explains the options.</p>

<h2>Rentals, second homes, and businesses</h2>
<p>Freeze losses at properties that are not occupied every day deserve extra attention. Short-term rentals on the islands, seasonal homes, and closed businesses may sit unheated during a cold snap, and nobody may notice a leak until days later. Some policies have specific conditions for vacant or unoccupied buildings, including heat and water shutoff requirements, so read those sections before winter. Owners of rental properties should also check whether loss of rental income is covered. Landlords and tenants should agree on who reports the claim and who documents tenant belongings, since tenant contents are usually covered under the tenant's own renters policy. Our <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential property claims</a> page covers how these situations are usually organized.</p>

<h2>Preparing for the next freeze</h2>
<p>A little preparation can prevent a claim. Before a hard freeze, insulate exposed pipes and hose bibs, open cabinet doors under sinks on exterior walls, let faucets drip if advised, and know how to shut off the main water. For vacant properties and rentals, keep the heat on or drain the system, and document what you did. Take photos of your home's condition now so you have a "before" record if something happens.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>Serving Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities, Corpus Christi Public Adjuster helps homeowners and businesses document water damage, prepare detailed estimates for tear-out and rebuild, and present the claim to the insurer. We cannot promise a particular result, and coverage depends on your policy. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

